{:edb/db-before-t 1497 :edb/db-after-t 1498 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534810 50 #inst "2026-10-12T16:00:23.596Z" 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186102662 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102667 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186105735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436856 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186105735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436859 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186105735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436860 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186105735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436861 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186105737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436855 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186105737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436857 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186105737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436858 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186105737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436862 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186105963 1050 840.0 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186106131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186106131 1071 "Given that $a = ⟨0,-2, 3⟩$, $b = ⟨4,-2, 1⟩$, and $c = ⟨3,-1, 2⟩$, find $a \\cdot (b \\cdot c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." 13194139534810 17592186045417 false] [17592186106131 1071 "Given that $a=⟨0,-2,3⟩,$ $b=⟨4,-2,1⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,-1,2⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186106131 1074 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))i-(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)j+(4\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-3i-5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨0,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-5,2⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186107076 1064 "The scalar triple product has two important properties.\n\nFirst, scalars can be factored out of the product. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{a}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot ({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{c}) = {\\color{red}2}\\left[ \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\right].\n$$\n\nSecond, if we swap exactly two vectors in the scalar triple product, the result changes sign. For example, swapping $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\cdot ( {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = - {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\cdot ( {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n$$\n\nNote that if we swap twice, then the result remains the same, because the negatives cancel out. For example, swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}$ and then swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-b\\cdot (a\\times c) \\\\  & =-(-b\\cdot (c\\times a)) \\\\  & =b\\cdot (c\\times a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese properties arise naturally from the fact that the scalar triple product can be computed as a determinant.\n\n- If we multiply a row of the matrix by a number, then the determinant is multiplied by that number:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1} & 2{a}_{2} & 2{a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\n- If we swap two rows, then the determinant changes sign:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\n$$" 13194139534810 17592186045417 false] [17592186107076 1064 "The scalar triple product has two important properties.\n\nFirst, scalars can be factored out of the product. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{a}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot ({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{c}) = {\\color{red}2}\\left[ \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\right].\n$$\n\nSecond, if we swap exactly two vectors in the scalar triple product, the result changes sign. For example, swapping $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\cdot ( {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = - {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}} \\cdot ( {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n$$\n\nNote that if we swap twice, then the result remains the same, because the negatives cancel out. For example, swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}$ and then swapping $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-b\\cdot (a\\times c) \\\\  & =-(-b\\cdot (c\\times a)) \\\\  & =b\\cdot (c\\times a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese properties arise naturally from the fact that the scalar triple product can be computed as a determinant.\n\n1\\. If we multiply a row of the matrix by a number, then the determinant is multiplied by that number:\n  ![](images/82/827b78de142e085b00c9c7eccb43ba32b30861ef45d91b53b1f35c86a67b78f4.svg)\n\n2\\. If we swap two rows, then the determinant changes sign:\n  ![](images/ac/acadadda23e53448493a31441e2d3e53e89b6a320d6b4040c7567a2489d3cff3.svg)" 13194139534810 17592186045417 true] [17592186408071 1071 "Let $r=⟨-4,-1,2⟩$, $q=⟨-2,3,1⟩$ and $p=⟨5,-2,-1⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ using a determinant.\n\n$r\\cdot (q\\times p)=$\n\n$-21$" 13194139534810 17592186045417 false] [17592186408071 1071 "Let $r=⟨-4,-1,2⟩$, $q=⟨-2,3,1⟩$ and $p=⟨5,-2,-1⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ using a determinant.\n\n$r\\cdot (q\\times p)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534810 1759218604